  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The chance of rain will gradually increase today \nfrom northwest to southeast, with showers continuing tonight across \nWestern NY.\n\nA sharpening mid level trough will continue to dig southeast across \nQuebec today, while a strong ridge builds over much of the central \nUS. The interaction between these two large scale features will \ncontinue to result in thermal packing and frontogenesis across the \nGreat Lakes today, with a northwest to southeast oriented frontal \nzone strengthening across the Great Lakes. \n\nFrontogenesis and moisture pooling will increase from northwest to \nsoutheast today, while passing mid level shortwaves in the northwest \nflow aloft bring periods of enhanced ascent. The net influence of \nthese features will result in a northwest to southeast increase in \nrain chances today, with the first of the showers arriving near the \nCanadian border this morning, then peaking in coverage late this \nafternoon. Extensive cloud cover will hold down instability, with \nonly a slight chance of a few isolated weak thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening.\n\nThe strong mid level trough will continue to dig southeast across \nnorthern New England and the Canadian Maritimes tonight, with \nassociated flow adjustments in its wake forcing the frontal zone to \nmove south and west. This will focus additional showers across \nWestern NY overnight, while areas from Rochester and the Finger \nLakes eastward become mainly dry with a temporary influx of drier \nmid level air moving south out of Canada. Forecast soundings suggest \nplenty of low level moisture pooling overnight beneath a shallow \nfrontal inversion, supporting areas of fog after midnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather will continue through much of the \nweek with occasional rounds of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nThe northwest to southeast oriented frontal zone will remain draped \nacross the Great Lakes much of the upcoming week, keeping the \npattern unsettled. The frontal zone will oscillate back and forth \nacross the area several times over the course of the week, with the \nfrontal position modulated by passing mid level shortwaves and also \nthe influence of composite outflow and cold pools from rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms.\n\nMonday through Monday night, the frontal zone will gradually move \nback northeast across the area, with a commensurate southwest to \nnortheast increase in rain chances. Instability remains quite \nlimited during this period, with just a slight chance of a few \nisolated weak thunderstorms. \n\nThunderstorm chances will increase by midweek as an EML plume \nadvects ENE through the Ohio Valley and southern Great Lakes, \nsupporting a reservoir of strong instability just upstream. The most \nactive weather will be along the northern edge of the EML where \ncapping is weaker, and it appears this corridor may setup from \nMichigan into NY/PA around midweek. Forecast instability and shear \nprofiles suggest severe weather may be a concern midweek, with the \nrisk peaking on Wednesday. It will be several more days before the \nmesoscale details of the evolution start to become more apparent, \nwhich will drive the details of the severe weather risk.\n\nModel guidance suggests the frontal zone will move back south and \nwest by Thursday and Friday, with rain chances gradually diminishing \nacross the eastern Great Lakes.",
